# Technical scoring rubric M01–M10

This English reference follows sections 1–5 of the technical self-check scoring proposal. The [Chinese export](技术M量表.md) and its [provenance](技术M量表.provenance.json) retain the original wording, source and export hashes, and section selection. Apply the [shared conventions](共同约定.md) and effective decisions for the current task.

## 1. Report three assessments separately

| Assessment | Question | Result |
| --- | --- | --- |
| SOP project design | Are requirements, technical design, implementation design, and acceptance design supported, understandable, and aligned for this version? | Ten design scores, review status, critical blockers, and pending decisions. |
| Actual product | Did the real operations and assertions pass on the specified deliverable version and platform? | Report actual pass rate, unverified and failed cases, environment blockers, human-test ratio, and evidence separately. |
| Self-check engine | Can it find known defects, identify real blockers, and avoid incorrect closure? | Evaluate recall, valid finding ratio, missed critical issues, and false blockers separately. |

Keep executable test plans, external research, model self-review, and teaching examples at their actual evidence level. Use real execution for claims about product passing. The ten weights, fields, calculation, and proposed threshold are project design choices. [arc42 quality scenarios](https://docs.arc42.org/section-10/) and [NASA software design analysis](https://swehb.nasa.gov/spaces/SWEHBVD/pages/102695748/8.55%2B-%2BSoftware%2BDesign%2BAnalysis)

## 2. Pin objects before creating scoring units

### 2.1 Current baseline

Record the project, requirement, design, task, and acceptance versions, rule version, object inventory version, target platforms, effective user decisions, and pending confirmations. Give each object a stable ID, exact location, and revision. A file can also have a content hash.

Determine the full inventory from requirements, delivery scope, and technical design. Register a missing required interface as an object linked to its requirement and create its check rows. Keep its missing content and applicable rows visible.

Inventory requirements, design objects, modules, interfaces, data objects, required scenarios, key technical hypotheses, quality goals, security boundaries, dependencies, deployment designs, acceptance criteria and target platform pairs, cited sources, and earlier and current issues.

If the completeness of a class cannot be established, leave the dimension unscorable and record that the inventory is undetermined. Pin a new inventory version after identifying more objects.

### 2.2 Reproducible scoring units

Each unit is one fixed check row with key `scoring baseline ID + dimension ID + object ID + platform or scope ID + check item ID`.

Expand the rows as specified below. All necessary conditions of one check item must be satisfied for the row to count as satisfied. Change the rule version and regenerate the units whenever adding, removing, or changing the granularity of rules.

| Field | Required record |
| --- | --- |
| Unit ID, dimension ID, and check item ID | Unique location and applicable rule version. |
| Object ID, version, location, and platform or scope | Exact content under review. |
| Applicability and reason | Applicable, reviewed inapplicable, or pending review. Give a scope basis for exclusions. |
| Satisfaction conditions | Pinned required fields and content judgments. |
| Four-state result | Satisfied, unsatisfied, unchecked, or inapplicable. |
| Evidence ID and location | Evidence entry, supported conclusion, and object version. |
| Reviewer and time | Actual reviewer or tool, rule or tool version, and time. |
| Semantic reviewer and conclusion | Reviewer or model, service, version and run ID. Identify whether the reviewer also authored the content. |
| Issue and recheck links | Issue ID, blocker and reason, revision and recheck records. If none were found, record that finding and its review basis. |

Satisfaction requires locatable current content, applicable evidence, and the review the rule requires. Verify responsibilities, trace meaning, contract agreement, and source applicability through semantic findings and reasons. Record model review, independent human review, and actual trials under their own identities.

For evidence, record ID, type, file or address and location, creation time, supported assertion, object ID and version, platform and environment, source or creator, limits, and current or expired status. Use applicable source material for facts, review records for design findings, and actual execution for behavior.

